  "body": "Currently, when a form is submitted using the `submit()` method, it is not made clear in the specification whether a form's entry list should be constructed before or after subsequent code should be executed. This has led to a difference in implementation- when testing on Safari 12, Firefox 64, and Chrome 76, Chrome and Firefox construct the entry list immediately, whereas Safari executes subsequent code before constructing the entry list. Therefore, if subsequent code alters the contents of the form, the form's resulting entry list is different.\r\n\r\nTo reproduce:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!doctype html>\r\n<html>\r\n  <head></head>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <form>\r\n      <button id=\"btn\" type=\"submit\" >submit</button>\r\n      <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"a\" />\r\n    </form>\r\n\r\n    <script>\r\n      document.forms[0].addEventListener('submit', e => {\r\n        document.forms[0].submit();\r\n        document.querySelector('input').remove();\r\n        e.preventDefault();\r\n      });\r\n    </script>\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nChrome and Firefox go to `...?a= `, whereas Safari goes to `...?`",
